<title>MALAWI: Malawi says EU offering help to rebuild roads.</title>
<headline>Malawi says EU offering help to rebuild roads.</headline>
<dateline>BLANTYRE, Malawi 1997-07-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>The European Union (EU) has offered 261 million kwacha ($17.4 million) to help pay for rebuilding and maintaining Malawi's poor road network, President Bakili Muluzi said.</p>
<p>He told a rally in Lilongwe on Sunday to mark the country's independence day that the offer was intended to keep on track a programme to rehabilitate the country's 15,000 km (9,400 miles) of road.</p>
<p>&quot;I take the condition of our roads very seriously. I am pleased to announce that the European Union has agreed to make available to Malawi government, 261 million kwacha to help us catch up with the road maintenance problem we inherited,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>Graders have already begun work scraping and levelling badly pot-holed roads in Blantyre, the commercial capital.</p>
</text>
